                    },
                    {
                        "header": "Process",
                        "subsections": [
                            {
                                "header": "Data collection",
                                "displayStyle": "row-right",
                                "description": ["In order to isomorphically translate UbuWeb to UbuTube, the archive must be archived. The UbuTube crawler scrapes UbuWeb's 3000+ film and artist pages from on a pruned list of URLs. The UbuTube parser takes that content, removes extraneous HTML, and preserves it in a database. Film objects are linked to artist objects based on authorship."],
                                "images": [
                                    {
                                        "type": "column",
                                        "srcs": [
                                            {
                                                "src": "/case_studies/ubutube/process_4.mp4"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                    
                                ]
                            },
                            {
				                "header": "Experience design",
                                "displayStyle": "row-left",
                                "description": [
                                    "UbuWeb's emphasis on fluidity and transience prompted me to experiment with UbuTube's aesthetic structuration. I wanted the design to reflect the original website's ideology in the same manner that its content does. The ideal, I thought, would be a website that rejects all conventions of web design and radically escapes towards other zones of expression.",
                                    "The first draft took the form of a disparate array of text blocks vying for space upon an undifferentiated white plane. The reasoning behind this deconstruction was to promote the hegemony of the filmic content over the non-filmic digital container. The negation of the container, however, does not remove the container, but, like an inverted glove, relegates the content to the container's negative. This difficult loop prompted a turn towards alternate avenues of escape--transforming the container rather than negating it."
                                ],
                                "images": [
                                    {
                                        "type": "slider",
                                        "srcs": [
                                            {
                                                "src": "/case_studies/ubutube/process_1.png"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "src": "/case_studies/ubutube/process_5.png",
                                                "caption": "First iteration of an UbuTube film page."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "displayStyle": "row-right",
                                "description": [
                                    "Instead of dissolving the container, we transform it. UbuTube becomes a <i>Cahiers du Cinéma</i> in miniature, a mimesis of print media lacking a target for its mimicry. The typography is totally uniform. Dividing borders demarcate coassociative textual zones like newspaper classifieds. The fixed outer margin suggests another medium: UbuTube is not a website, but rather a kind of pamphlet to be flipped through. The page-like navigation animations on the mobile interface are the most explicit expression of this.",
                                    "As in a periodical, the table of contents is brought to the fore, ever-present on the right-hand side of the page. Any one of UbuTube's 3000+ pages is quickly and readily accessible from any other. Everything the user might need is available to them at any moment, but what the user actually wants--the film itself--constantly occupies the horizon of the gaze."
                                ],
                                "images": [
                                    {
                                        "type": "slider",
                                        "srcs": [
                                            {
                                                "src": "/case_studies/ubutube/process_7.png"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "src": "/case_studies/ubutube/process_8.png"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "header": "Black Square / Spiral",
                                "displayStyle": "row-left",
                                "description": [
                                    "There are no images on the UbuTube website, save for two. The home page features a large reproduction of Kazimir Malevich's <i>Black Square</i>, while a small spiral--reproduced in the favicon--occupies the top left corner.",
                                    "Malevich intended to separate art from imitation by neglecting artistic content and pursuing a radical supremacy of form. However, this dispossession inadvertently gave rise to a new kind of content synonymous with its own expression. <i>Black Square</i> is replete with content, but it is not symbolicating. Like a black hole collapsing on itself, what the painting <i>is</i> becomes inseparable from what it <i>does</i>.",
                                    "Given the manner in which UbuWeb's films follow this avant-gardist tradition, I felt that it would be proper for their container to do the same. Each film has preexisting layers of content and form within itself, but when considered in the context of UbuTube, these layers fold into a singular content for which the site itself becomes the principal expressive medium. Instead of subjugating the content to the medium, we do the opposite: the site's expressive form becomes subjugated to its content, achieving an teleologically identical kind of synthesis. Everything is now directed towards the film, which sucks in the gaze of the user like the black hole of the black square.",
                                    "UbuTube's minor symbol is the spiral (<i>gidouille</i>), which is a direct allusion to Alfred Jarry's <i>Ubu Roi</i> (1896). The <i>gidouille</i> adorns the stomach of Jarry's protagonist, representing his boundless hunger and greed. The spiral logomark was chosen for its metonomy with UbuWeb/Tube. An 1965 television adaptation of <i>Ubu Roi</i> is viewable on <a href='https://ubu.com/film/jarry_ubu-averty.html' target='_blank'>both</a> <a href='https://ubutube.org/film/4186738431008895' target='_blank'>sites</a>."
                                ],
                                
